  "prediction": "The report provides a detailed history of the Appropriations Committees in the United States House of Representatives and Senate from 1946 to present, with a focus on the changes in subcommittee jurisdictions over the years. The report begins by outlining the creation of the Appropriations Committees and their role in the legislative process. It then describes how subcommittee jurisdictions have shifted over time in response to changing government needs and political dynamics.\n\nOne significant change occurred in the late 1960s when subcommittee jurisdictions were realigned to reflect the growing complexity of the federal budget. This led to the creation of subcommittees focused on specific areas such as defense, education, and transportation.\n\nThe report also highlights some notable events that influenced subcommittee jurisdictions, such as the creation of the Department of Energy in 1977 and the passage of the Defense Reorganization Act in 1986.\n\nIn more recent years, the report notes the impact of the 9/11 attacks on the Appropriations Committees, leading to the creation of a Select Intelligence Oversight Panel to oversee spending on intelligence activities. However, this panel was eliminated in 2011.\n\nThroughout the report, there is a focus on the importance of the Appropriations Committees in shaping the federal budget and ensuring that resources are allocated effectively and efficiently. The report concludes by highlighting the current jurisdictions of the subcommittees in the House and Senate.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report provides a detailed history of the Appropriations Committees in the United States House of Representatives and Senate from 1946 to present, with a focus on the changes in subcommittee jurisdictions over the years. The report begins by outlining the creation of the Appropriations Committees and their role in the legislative process. It then describes how subcommittee jurisdictions have shifted over time in response to changing government needs and political dynamics.\n\nOne significant change occurred in the late 1960s when subcommittee jurisdictions were realigned to reflect the growing complexity of the federal budget. This led to the creation of subcommittees focused on specific areas such as defense, education, and transportation.\n\nThe report also highlights some notable events that influenced subcommittee jurisdictions, such as the creation of the Department of Energy in 1977 and the passage of the Defense Reorganization Act in 1986.\n\nIn more recent years, the report notes the impact of the 9/11 attacks on the Appropriations Committees, leading to the creation of a Select Intelligence Oversight Panel to oversee spending on intelligence activities. However, this panel was eliminated in 2011.\n\nThroughout the report, there is a focus on the importance of the Appropriations Committees in shaping the federal budget and ensuring that resources are allocated effectively and efficiently. The report concludes by highlighting the current jurisdictions of the subcommittees in the House and Senate.",
